endle","x":"2021","y":67.2},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2022","y":69.2},{"z":"Pendle","x":"2023","y":53}],"selected":"Bolton","padding-top":"20","padding-left":"35","padding-right":"100","padding-bottom":"28","yMin":"40","ySuffix":"%","note":"Figures are based on a small number of survey responses in each area and are less precise than national or regional estimates. Ranking, and changes in ranking, may be the result of sampling variability."},{"id":"employment-level","content":"<p>Overall, there were around 120,000 people aged 16 and over in Bolton who were employed in the year ending December 2023. This is down from the previous year, when there were around 128,000 people aged 16 and over who were employed.</p><p>Local population changes mean that the numbers of people in each area who are employed, unemployed and economically inactive in this article may be under- or over-estimates.</p>"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"unemployment","title":"Unemployment fell slightly in Bolton compared with the previous year","content":"<p>Unemployment estimates are modelled to improve their precision compared to those based only on responses provided via the Annual Population Survey.</p><p>Around 6,600 people aged 16 and over in Bolton were unemployed in the year ending December 2023. This is a rate of 5.3%.</p><p>This was a slight decrease compared with the year ending  December 2022 when the unemployment rate was 5.4%.</p><p>Across the North West, from the year ending  December 2022 to the year ending December 2023, there was a decrease in the unemployment rate from 4.1% to 3.8%.</p><p>Year on year, the number of people unemployed in the North West fell from around 146,000 to around 137,000 over the same period.</p><p>Unemployment across Great Britain rose slightly between the year ending December 2022 and the year ending  December 2023, going from around 1,180,000 people (3.6%) to around 1,240,000 (3.7%).</p><p>Unemployed people are those without jobs who are actively seeking work and available to take up a job. Some jobless people may not seek work or be able to work for various reasons, such as retirement, sickness, or study, and are called \"economically inactive\". </p><p>This means that the employment rate and the unemployment rate can both go up at the same time as people move into the labour market and become job seekers.</p><p>The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed people out of everyone \"economically active\" in the labour market, that is, everyone who is employed or looking for work. The employment rate is out of the working-age population including  \"economically inactive\" people. </p>"},{"id":"chart-highest","type":"Chart","chartType":"bar-highlight","title":"Highest unemployment rates (%) of areas in the North West","subtitle":"Among those aged 16 and over and active in the labour market, year ending December 2023","footer":"Source: Model-based estimates of unemployment from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"x":8.1,"y":"Pendle"},{"x":7,"y":"Liverpool"},{"x":5.3,"y":"Bolton"},{"x":5.2,"y":"Salford"},{"x":5.1,"y":"Manchester"}],"selected":"Bolton","padding-left":"120","xSuffix":"%"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"claimant-count","title":"Claimant Count in Bolton was higher than in the previous year","content":"<p>The <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\" rel=\"external\">Claimant Count</a> is an experimental statistic that measures the number of people who are claiming unemployment-related benefits.</p><p>The Claimant Count in Bolton was 10825 in March 2024, up from March 2023, when there were 10140 claimants.</p><p>The proportion of people aged 16 to 64 years in Bolton who were claiming unemployment-related benefits was 6.0% in March 2024, an increase compared with March 2023 when it was 5.6%.</p>"},{"id":"chart-claimanttime","type":"Chart","chartType":"line-highlight","title":"Claimant Count (%) for areas in the North West","subtitle":"Among those aged 16 to 64 years, March 2014 to March 2024","footer":"Source: Claimant Count from Department for Work and Pensions","data":[{"z":"Manchester","x":"2014","y":4.3},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2015","y":2.8},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2016","y":2.8},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2017","y":2.6},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2018","y":2.9},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2019","y":4},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2020","y":4.5},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2021","y":9.4},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2022","y":6.6},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2023","y":6.1},{"z":"Manchester","x":"2024","y":6.5},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2014","y":2.5},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2015","y":1.8},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2016","y":1.9},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2017","y":2.4},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2018","y":2.9},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2019","y":3},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2020","y":3.1},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2021","y":5.5},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2022","y":3.5},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2023","y":3.2},{"z":"Lancaster","x":"2024","y":3},{"z":"Preston","x":"2014","y":2.9},{"z":"Preston","x":"2015","y":2.1},{"z":"Preston","x":"2016","y":2.2},{"z":"Preston","x":"2017","y":2.3},{"z":"Preston","x":"2018","y":2.4},{"z":"Preston","x":"2019","y":3.2},{"z":"Preston","x":"2020","y":3.8},{"z":"Preston","x":"2021","y":6.6},{"z":"Preston","x":"2022","y":4.5},{"z":"Preston","x":"2023","y":4.5},{"z":"Preston","x":"2024","y":5},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2014","y":5.1},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2015","y":3.8},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2016","y":3.5},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2017","y":3.4},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2018","y":3.5},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2019","y":3.9},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2020","y":4.8},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2021","y":9.2},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2022","y":6.4},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2023","y":5.9},{"z":"Liverpool","x":"2024","y":5.8},{"z":"Fylde","x":"2014","y":2},{"z":"Fylde","x":"2015","y":1.4},{"z":"Fylde","x":"2016","y":1.4},{